<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in GMT to EEST Conversions</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>1. Confusing GMT with British Summer Time (BST)</h3>
    <p>
      During summer, London observes BST (UTC+1), which is 2 hours behind EEST (UTC+3). Standard GMT (UTC+0) remains 3 hours behind EEST. Always verify whether your UK team uses GMT or BST.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>2. Conflating EET with EEST</h3>
    <p>
      EET is UTC+2 (winter), while EEST is UTC+3 (summer). Using winter offsets during summer months introduces an unwanted 1-hour error into scheduled events.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>3. Assuming Turkey Follows European DST Rules</h3>
    <p>
      Turkey permanently uses Turkey Time (TRT, UTC+3) year-round and does not adjust clocks in autumn. Although TRT matches EEST during summer, Turkey does not fall back to EET in winter.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>4. Date Rollover Errors on Late Evening GMT Times</h3>
    <p>
      Converting 10:00 PM GMT to EEST yields 1:00 AM on the following day. Failing to note the date shift can lead to missed late-night server maintenance windows.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- TECHNICAL TIMEZONE DATABASE SECTION -->
<section class="content" id="technical-details" aria-labelledby="tech-heading">
  <h2 id="tech-heading">Technical Specifications & IANA Timezone Database Metadata</h2>
  <p>
    Engineers building international enterprise applications require exact tzdb keys for precise timestamp arithmetic.
  </p>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Canonical Identifier</h3>
      <p>The primary IANA time zone identifier for EEST is <code>Europe/Athens</code>. Standard aliases include <code>Europe/Helsinki</code>, <code>Europe/Kyiv</code>, and <code>Europe/Bucharest</code>.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>ISO 8601 Offset Representation</h3>
      <p>EEST is formatted in ISO 8601 strings as <code>+03:00</code> (e.g., <code>2026-07-30T15:00:00+03:00</code>).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>POSIX Timezone Specification</h3>
      <p>The standard POSIX string for EEST is <code>EET-2EEST,M3.5.0/3,M10.5.0/4</code>, defining exact March and October clock transitions.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>EU Harmonization Directive</h3>
      <p>All EU member states in the EEST zone shift clocks simultaneously at 01:00 UTC under European Parliament Directive 2000/84/EC.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

